{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 argues that a single evaluation cannot cleanly attribute reliability to the checklist because panel-level intelligence can override or supplement the checklist, but it advances no causal mechanism for the critical step: how extra-checklist reasoning would be elicited and operate under the single-evaluation constraint. The analogy's 'transfer_claims' merely state that one would probe the system with divergent items and 'see which one the system tracks,' but SRC_001 identifies no causal variable or process linking such a probe to observed behavior. It also lacks any mechanism for why the panel's introspective self-reports would be causally valid evidence rather than post-hoc rationalization. The claim is framed against attribution within a single evaluation, yet the only mechanism it gestures at is a behavioral probe whose causal interpretation is left unjustified — it does not show why accuracy on adversarial items would causally implicate 'extra-checklist reasoning' rather than, e.g., the checklist's deeper application. Without specifying the causal chain from the panel's implicit capacity to the observed outcome, the confound claim is an assertion, not an argument.","target":"01599486936644d2dfb84f90dedb28de71102b9340f4d239d13070cc0ee26e34"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's criterion for distinguishing genuine reliability from compliance is the inclusion of adversarial items whose surface cues 'decouple from the underlying causal structure,' but it never supplies the causal mechanism by which the checklist itself would encode or track that causal structure. The analogy to diagnostic tests is disanalogous in exactly the causally decisive respect: a diagnostic test can be validated by a gold standard external to the test and to the test population, whereas SRC_002 itself notes that adversarial items in evaluation are constructed by 'the same process that defines genuine reliability,' risking circularity — yet it treats decoupling probes as nonetheless discriminating. It does not identify what causal feature of a checklist would let genuine reliability 'survive' decoupling probes; it simply predicts sustained accuracy. The claim conflates the causal basis of the checklist's reliability with the checklist's correlation with outcome-determining structure, but offers no mechanism explaining why a checklist that genuinely encodes causal structure would perform differently on adversarial items than one that merely tracks reliable cues. Without that mechanism, the 'if and only if' criterion is unsupported.","target":"08d88c7736874a6e7721748e8c1b2df087022409300b52f511a0b0298e37faf9"}]}